WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.633
PPO.

2
00:00:01.933 --> 00:00:04.819
One small trick keeps policy training stable.

3
00:00:04.819 --> 00:00:07.833
It is also the engine behind aligning chatbots.

4
00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:14.967
Policy-gradient methods adjust the policy's weights directly, making good actions more probable.

5
00:00:15.267 --> 00:00:18.733
Their curse: one oversized update can wreck the policy.

6
00:00:19.033 --> 00:00:23.567
And that broken policy collects the next batch of data, so it may never recover.

7
00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:27.767
PPO's one elegant move starts with a ratio.

8
00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:33.200
New policy probability divided by old policy probability, for the same action.

9
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.233
Then we clip that ratio to a narrow band.

10
00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:38.367
Epsilon is about zero point two.

11
00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:43.200
Here is the clip as curves, for a good action and for a bad one.

12
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.300
On the left, a good action.

13
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:50.567
The dashed line is the raw objective, growing without limit as the ratio rises.

14
00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:56.182
The clipped version goes flat beyond one point two, so pushing further earns nothing.

15
00:00:56.182 --> 00:00:59.933
On the right, a bad action goes flat below zero point eight.

16
00:01:00.233 --> 00:01:03.663
Past the band's edge, a sample simply stops pulling.

17
00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:09.533
It's a soft brake, not a wall: across epochs and minibatches, ratios can still overshoot.

18
00:01:10.733 --> 00:01:13.933
Two reasons this is the most important algorithm here.

19
00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:17.667
It is the default for continuous control and robotics.

20
00:01:17.967 --> 00:01:20.244
And it is the engine of RLHF.

21
00:01:20.244 --> 00:01:27.467
The LLM is the policy, tokens are actions, and a reward model stands in for the environment.

22
00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:31.633
In Stable Baselines3 it's a single parameter.

23
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:33.833
Clip range of zero point two.

24
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:39.670
In the logs, watch clip fraction: the share of samples where the clip actually engaged.

25
00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:43.933
Healthy runs sit between about zero point one and zero point three.

26
00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:48.133
Remember the four models of RLHF?

27
00:01:48.433 --> 00:01:51.090
Policy, reference, reward, and value.

28
00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:54.033
The optimizer holding it together is PPO.

29
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:58.590
The clip keeps each update close to the policy that sampled the batch.

30
00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:03.333
A KL penalty against the frozen reference model stops reward-hacked gibberish.

31
00:02:03.633 --> 00:02:05.867
Build intuition on LunarLander first.

32
00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:07.827
Let's recap.

33
00:02:07.827 --> 00:02:10.233
Big policy updates can wreck learning.

34
00:02:10.533 --> 00:02:13.767
PPO clips the probability ratio to a trust band.

35
00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:18.267
It's the default for control, and the engine of RLHF.

36
00:02:18.567 --> 00:02:21.280
Watch the clip fraction in your logs.

37
00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:22.233
Next up: A2C.
